#section("II. Where They Rhyme")

#subsection("The asymptotic self")

The strongest connection lies in the second _Critique_. Kant argues that morality
demands the complete conformity of the will with the moral law — holiness — a
conformity no finite, embodied rational being can achieve at any single moment
@kant-cpracr[Ak. 5:122]. The moral law commands this holiness unconditionally,
without lowering its demand to what a creature can presently meet
@kant-cpracr[Ak. 5:32]. From the gap between the command and the creature Kant
draws not despair but a postulate: because the demand is unconditional and the
achievement is impossible in a moment, practical reason requires an endless
progress toward the perfect fitness of the will — and this endless progress in
turn requires the immortality of the soul as its condition @kant-cpracr[Ak. 5:122].
Perfection, for Kant, is not a state one snaps into but a trajectory one is set
upon and continues indefinitely, approaching the standard asymptotically forever.

The structure is strikingly close to eternal progression, and it is worth noting
that the scriptural traditions the Latter-day Saints inherit already speak in the
same asymptotic register. The Sermon on the Mount issues the command to perfection
as a _standard_ — "be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect"
@bible[Matthew 5:48] — not as a report that perfection has been reached; and the
prophetic idiom of growth "line upon line, precept upon precept"
@bible[Isaiah 28:10] describes exactly the incremental, unfinished ascent Kant's
postulate formalizes. Where Kant reasons his way to endless moral progress from the
structure of obligation, the tradition simply commands the ascent and describes its
manner. The convergence is not on a doctrine but on a _shape_: perfection set as a
standard toward which one is always advancing, never a possession one already holds.

#subsection("The uncreated self")

There is a second, deeper resonance, and it concerns the nature of the self.
Kant's transcendental idealism locates the real self — the free, noumenal self —
outside the causal, spatiotemporal order altogether. Appearances are governed by
natural necessity, but the self as it is in itself is not merely one more link in
that chain @kant-cpr[A538/B566]; freedom is secured precisely because the self is
not, at bottom, a product of nature @kant-cpr[A546/B574]. The move is a refusal:
the self that acts is not wholly constituted by the deterministic order that
constitutes everything it can observe, and only that refusal makes genuine moral
agency intelligible.

Latter-day Saint theology, following the King Follett discourse, makes a
structurally parallel refusal. The core of personhood — what the discourse calls
"intelligence" — is held to be uncreated and co-eternal with God, never having had
a beginning and never having been made @king-follett. Both moves do the same
philosophical work: they ground genuine moral agency by refusing to let the self be
entirely constituted or determined by an external creator or a deterministic causal
order. Kant secures freedom by placing the noumenal self beyond nature; the King
Follett discourse secures it by placing the intelligence beyond creation. The
metaphysical vocabularies could hardly be more different — one critical and
epistemically austere, the other confidently revelatory — but the target is the
same: a self that is genuinely the origin of its own acts, because it is not, in
the relevant sense, the product of anything else.
